Reranker-lane busy-degrade, for the Hindsight HA add-on.

Applied in the Dockerfile with `patch -p1 -d /app/api` against the API package
COPY-ed from the digest-pinned upstream image (v0.10.2 / 5fc4ce20). When
bumping HINDSIGHT_IMAGE, regenerate this patch against the new source — the
build fails loudly here if the context drifts.

Problem (measured on the N150, 2026-07-20): the local cross-encoder rerank is
the serialized section of every recall — ~6.5s per 100-pair job with
RERANKER_LOCAL_MAX_CONCURRENT=1 — and the executor queue is FIFO. A 6-deep
interactive burst 504'd at 66% (measured: 7.4s, 13.8s, then 4x 504@15s),
with the CPU already saturated (~390% of 400%), so raising the reranker
concurrency would only thrash.

(Until add-on v0.4.x this patch also routed consolidation's internal recalls
off the cross-encoder lane. Upstream v0.9+ does that natively — consolidation
recall passes reranking="interleave" and never touches the cross-encoder — so
that half was dropped when rebasing onto v0.10.2.)

One change, a no-op unless its env var is set:

engine/memory_engine.py + engine/cross_encoder.py busy-degrade: when
HINDSIGHT_API_RERANKER_BUSY_PASSTHROUGH_THRESHOLD=N (>0) and the local
reranker lane already has >= N unfinished jobs, an interactive recall
switches to upstream's native reranking="rrf" path (fusion order,
passthrough scoring, scores.reranker=None in the response) instead of
queueing toward a certain 504 — degraded ranking beats an "unavailable"
outcome for consumers. Occupancy is tracked in LocalSTCrossEncoder:
incremented on submit in the event loop, released by a done-callback on the
concurrent future, which fires exactly once for normal completion, worker
exception, or cancellation of a still-queued job — and NOT before a started
thread actually finishes, so doomed post-deadline work stays visible to the
check and a cancelled queued job cannot leak the counter.
Atomicity of check-then-submit: the check sits after the last await of
recall step 4 (hydrate_results); the reranker initializes eagerly at startup
(upstream removed the lazy option), the local provider carries no retry
wrapper, and pair building is synchronous, so there is no event-loop
suspension between the pending_jobs() check and the submit increment; the
API and worker share one event loop.
The build runs build-smoke/rerank_occupancy_smoke.py as a regression gate
for the accounting.

